%! Parameters with types and defaults.
%!
%! Parameters give names to values that a program uses, so that they can
%! be changed in one place. A parameter can have a type, and it can be
%! computed from other parameters. The value in the program is a default:
%! a program that uses this one as a module can set another value, and
%! so can the command line.
%!
%! The example shows:
%! - how to define parameters with @parameter,
%! - how to give a parameter a type,
%! - how to use parameters in rule heads, conditions and other parameters.

@parameter $limit: int = 3 .
@parameter $label: string = "item" .
@parameter $double = $limit * 2 .

n(1) .
n(2) .
n(3) .
n(4) .

small(?x) :- n(?x), ?x < $limit .
labeled(?x, $label) :- n(?x), ?x = $limit .
doubled(?x, $double) :- n(?x), ?x > $limit .

@output small, labeled, doubled .
